SECTION 4 — LAYER 3: TRADE SECRET STRATEGY

4.1   WHAT IS A TRADE SECRET
A Trade Secret is any confidential business information that gives a competitive advantage — it is protected as long as it remains secret. Unlike patents (which expire in 20 years), trade secrets can last forever. The Coca-Cola formula has been a trade secret since 1891. KEYSTONE's data schema and governance logic are ideal trade secret candidates — they are valuable precisely because competitors do not know their structure.
4.2   KEYSTONE TRADE SECRET ASSETS

Asset	What It Contains	Why It Must Stay Secret
The Eternal Seed Schema	Exact field structure, data hierarchy, and category taxonomy of the Seed	Competitors could replicate the system if schema is known
The Claw Payload Specification	What data each claw carries to each node, in what format, at what trigger	Reveals the protocol for context distribution
The Session Restore Protocol Logic	Exact sequence of operations on session initialization and state load	Loading order and conflict resolution logic is proprietary
The Memory Constitution Rules	Immutable truth set — rules governing what Seed considers authoritative	Contradiction resolution rules are the core of governance
