4.3   HOW TO PROTECT TRADE SECRETS — FIVE STEPS
STEP 1 — DOCUMENT EVERYTHING

Write down all trade secret assets with dates and authorship. Store in a secure, dated, access-controlled location. The White Paper v1.0 (May 29, 2026) is already an authorship anchor — but the schema details must remain in a SEPARATE, unpublished document.
STEP 2 — NON-DISCLOSURE AGREEMENTS (NDAS)

Every person who sees the schema, payload spec, or governance logic must sign an NDA before exposure. This includes contractors, developers, partners, and investors. An NDA breach gives legal recourse under the Defend Trade Secrets Act (DTSA).
STEP 3 — ACCESS CONTROL

Limit who can see each trade secret. The fewer people who know it, the stronger the legal protection. Implement need-to-know access protocols from Day 1.
STEP 4 — MARK EVERYTHING

Label all internal documents containing trade secrets:

Required Label — All Internal Documents
CONFIDENTIAL — TRADE SECRET — AXES CONTRACTING · KEYSTONE · NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION

STEP 5 — DO NOT PATENT TRADE SECRETS

Patent applications become public documents. If the Eternal Seed schema is included in a patent application, anyone in the world can read it. Trade secret protection is stronger for the schema — keep it entirely out of any patent filing.
4.4   NDA TEMPLATE NOTE
A standard mutual NDA should be drafted by an attorney and kept ready to deploy instantly whenever a new person is brought into the KEYSTONE development process. Budget $300–$500 for a custom NDA template from a business attorney.


